# Line endings.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Three files here are read by sh: the two hooks under
# .claude\hooks that Claude Code runs before a tool call, and .githooks\pre-commit.
# sh treats a carriage return as part of the word, so a CRLF copy dies on its first
# case statement before it reaches a single rule. Measured on 2026-09-18: the branch
# hook gives "Syntax error: word unexpected (expecting in)" and the paths hook gives
# "Syntax error: | unexpected".
#
# That is not a wall that fails open. sh exits 2 on a syntax error, and 2 is the code
# that REFUSES, so a CRLF checkout jams both walls shut and refuses every call rather
# than the protected ones, while the pre-commit stumbles past set -e and exits 0, which
# lets every commit through with no tests run.
#
# With no .gitattributes, Git for Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout, because its
# installer sets core.autocrlf to true. Proved on 2026-09-18 by cloning this repo with
# core.autocrlf=true, which checked all three out with CRLF line terminators.

# The default. Git decides what is text and stores it with LF.
* text=auto

# Read by sh. LF in the working tree on every machine, whatever core.autocrlf says.
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/pre-commit text eol=lf

# Evidence, never edited and never normalised. The run log came off a Windows machine
# and its line endings are part of what it records, the same as its contents.
steps/logs/** -text
samples/** -text
